<body><main>
<header><p class="eyebrow">AI Resource Hub / Research report / Draft</p><h1>The State of AI,<br /><span>November 2026</span></h1><p class="deck">More capable systems. More demanding decisions.</p><p>Evidence cut-off: <time datetime="2026-11-14">14 November 2026</time>. This is a mid-month assessment, not a forecast of the remaining fortnight.</p></header>
<section aria-labelledby="summary"><h2 id="summary">Executive summary</h2>
<p>November's releases make the choice of AI system more specific. Anthropic introduced Fable 5.2 and the access-controlled Mythos 5.2 on 9 November. Google widened access to Carbon on 10 November and introduced Hydrogen on 12 November. These are different offers for different workloads, with different costs and access conditions. <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/claude-fable-and-mythos-5-2">Anthropic announcement</a>; <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/gemini-4-carbon-now-available/">Carbon announcement</a>; <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/introducing-gemini-4-hydrogen/">Hydrogen announcement</a>.</p>
<p>Demand has an observable infrastructure footprint: NVIDIA reported US$96.221 billion in quarterly revenue for Q2 FY2027. Business adoption is broader too: the UK government's latest survey reports 32% using at least one AI technology, against 16% in the previous wave. Those figures describe different populations and periods. Neither measures how much useful work AI completes. <a href={data.charts[0].source}>NVIDIA results</a>; <a href={data.charts[1].source}>UK survey</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Our assessment:</strong> the useful question for 2027 is how much work a system can finish correctly, within an agreed budget and set of permissions. Buyers should ask for evidence from their own tasks, including checking time and failed attempts. Policymakers should ask who remains accountable when an agent acts. This report treats capability announcements, commercial results and observed adoption as separate kinds of evidence.</p>
</section>
<nav aria-label="Report contents"><ol><li><a href="#frontier">Frontier models and releases</a></li><li><a href="#weights">Open weights</a></li><li><a href="#agents">Agents and coding</a></li><li><a href="#compute">Compute and chips</a></li><li><a href="#money">Funding and revenue</a></li><li><a href="#policy">Policy and regulation</a></li><li><a href="#safety">Safety</a></li><li><a href="#adoption">Adoption</a></li><li><a href="#watch">What to watch in 2027</a></li></ol></nav>
<section><h2 id="frontier">01 / Frontier models and releases</h2>
<p>Anthropic's November update separates the underlying model from the safeguards and access offered with it. Fable 5.2 is available more broadly; Mythos 5.2 requires verification for uses such as cyber and life sciences. Anthropic lists standard token prices of US$8 per million input tokens and US$40 per million output tokens. These are provider prices, not a measured cost for completing a job. <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/release-notes/overview">Claude Platform release notes, 9 November</a>.</p>
<p>Google positions Carbon for difficult technical work and Hydrogen for faster execution across agent workflows. Carbon's wider release followed restricted access for cyber defenders. Hydrogen's launch lists a flat US$1.50 input and US$7.50 output price per million tokens across its million-token context window. Availability, speed and the amount of reasoning used all deserve attention alongside capability. <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/gemini-4-carbon-now-available/">Carbon release</a>; <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/introducing-gemini-4-hydrogen/">Hydrogen release</a>.</p>
<p>OpenAI's October rollout brought GPT-6 and interactive responses into ChatGPT. Its release notes also distinguish restricted life-sciences access for GPT-6 Rosalind Research from ordinary chat availability. A model name in a launch announcement therefore does not establish that every customer can use it through every product. <a href="https://openai.com/index/gpt-6-for-everyone/">GPT-6 rollout, 7 October</a>; <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/9624314-model-release-notes">OpenAI model release notes</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Assessment:</strong> there is no single winner established here. We have not run a common evaluation of these releases. For a purchase or migration, record the exact model identifier, access tier, tool permissions, inference settings and test date before comparing results.</p>
</section>
<section><h2 id="weights">02 / Open weights: a deployment decision</h2>
<p>Open weights remain a practical alternative to a hosted model. DeepSeek's April V4 preview supplied downloadable weights, and its October V4.1-Flash-1017 release also links a checkpoint. Qwen's Qwen3.8-27B card lists Apache 2.0 licensing and inference instructions. These are concrete artefacts to inspect, rather than evidence inferred from a product teaser. <a href="https://api-docs.deepseek.com/news/news260424/">DeepSeek V4 release</a>; <a href="https://api-docs.deepseek.com/news/news261017/">DeepSeek October release</a>; <a href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B">Qwen model card</a>.</p>
<p>The Gemma 4 technical report describes both dense and mixture-of-experts designs, with models ranging from 2.3 billion to 31 billion parameters. That range matters because an open model need not mean a frontier-scale infrastructure project. It also does not tell a buyer the memory or latency of a particular quantised deployment. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.02770">Gemma 4 technical report</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Assessment:</strong> evaluate the licence, checkpoint, runtime and hardware together. Downloadable weights do not by themselves establish that training data or a reproducible training recipe are available. Local control may be valuable for sensitive or specialised work, but the operating bill should include serving, patching, evaluation and incident response. Compare a real deployment with a real hosted endpoint, using the same acceptance criteria.</p>
</section>
<section><h2 id="agents">03 / Agents and coding: measure the completed job</h2>
<p>Tool orchestration is becoming part of the model offer. Anthropic describes Fable as an adviser to smaller models or an orchestrator that delegates work. DeepSeek's October release provides examples of project development and identifies supported coding harnesses. These are vendor descriptions and demonstrations, not independent measurements of a software team's productivity. <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/claude-fable-and-mythos-5-2">Fable release</a>; <a href="https://api-docs.deepseek.com/news/news261017/">DeepSeek release</a>.</p>
<p>Evidence from workplace experiments needs its own context. Demirer and colleagues studied random access to a coding assistant across three companies. Their design concerns an assistant suggesting code completions, rather than a controlled trial of every November agent. It should not be used to assign a productivity gain to a newly released model. <a href="https://www.mertdemirer.com/Papers/Demirer_AI_productivity.pdf">The Effects of Generative AI on High-Skilled Work</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Assessment:</strong> a credible agent trial should start with real issues and finish with accepted changes. Count elapsed time, failed attempts, review time and regressions. Keep rejected work in the denominator. For delegated work, record which model made each decision and what it could read or change. An impressive demonstration is a reason to test, not a reason to remove review.</p>
</section>
<section><h2 id="compute">04 / Compute and chips: the system around the accelerator</h2>
<p>NVIDIA's August results report US$89.0 billion of Data Center revenue in Q2 FY2027 and describe Vera Rubin ramping into full production. AMD's 12 November update covers MI430X, MI455X inference results and the introduction of MI450X on Helios. AMD's performance statements are vendor claims under its test conditions; this report does not turn them into an independent cross-vendor ranking. <a href={data.charts[0].source}>NVIDIA results and product update</a>; <a href="https://newsroom.amd.com/news/amd-advances-instinct-mi400-series-ai-scientific-computing/">AMD update</a>.</p>
<ReportBars {...data.charts[0]} />
<p><strong>Assessment:</strong> buyers should compare an available system at their required utilisation, precision and latency, including networking and serving software. Chip announcements and shipping capacity answer different questions. Revenue is useful evidence of the scale of the supplier business, but does not identify the training cost, utilisation or profitability of its customers.</p>
</section>
<section><h2 id="money">05 / Money: funding, revenue and obligations</h2>
<p>Anthropic announced a US$65 billion Series H at a US$965 billion post-money valuation in May. The announcement says the round included US$15 billion of previously committed hyperscaler investment. Adding those commitments to the headline round would double-count them. The valuation is a financing price, not sales or cash available to spend. <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h">Anthropic funding announcement</a>.</p>
<p>AP's 2 November account of Anthropic's updated IPO filing reports US$17 billion in quarterly revenue and a US$23.3 billion quarterly loss, as well as substantial future computing commitments. Revenue growth and profitability are separate questions. These are reported filing figures, not this report's audit of the accounts. <a href="https://apnews.com/article/0cbd1a649a4cce904be59af8f6b30179">AP on the updated filing</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Assessment:</strong> the figures support scrutiny rather than a sector-wide verdict. A funding round, a quarterly revenue figure, an annualised run rate and a multi-year capacity commitment cannot be added together. For 2027, watch whether revenue converts to cash after infrastructure and operating costs, and whether capacity agreements become delivered, useful compute. No market-wide funding total is estimated here.</p>
</section>
<section><h2 id="policy">06 / Policy and regulation: three different tracks</h2>
<h3>United Kingdom</h3><p>The government's agentic AI resilience factsheet, updated 3 November, describes a planned consultation on measures for regulated entities under proposed powers in the Cyber Security and Resilience Bill. Its focus includes loss of control over agents. This is a statement of intended policy development, not evidence that all proposed agent requirements are already in force. <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cyber-security-and-resilience-network-and-information-systems-bill-factsheets/agentic-ai-resilience-measures">UK factsheet</a>.</p>
<h3>European Union</h3><p>The Commission's current FAQ distinguishes existing prohibitions and AI-literacy provisions, applicable since February 2025, from general-purpose model obligations applicable since August 2025. It gives revised high-risk dates of 2 December 2027 and 2 August 2028 for systems embedded in physical products, following the Digital Omnibus. The old shorthand that all major requirements arrive in August 2026 is insufficient. <a href="https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/faqs/navigating-ai-act">Commission implementation FAQ</a>.</p>
<h3>United States</h3><p>The June executive order describes a policy of AI innovation and security through collaboration with industry. Digital.gov also records September's executive-branch terminology change to “Super Intelligence”, while noting that existing laws and past documents retain their wording. Terminology does not establish a scientific capability threshold or replace a legal analysis of a particular deployment. <a href="https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/eo-14409.pdf">June executive order</a>; <a href="https://digital.gov/topics/artificial-intelligence">Digital.gov policy record</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Assessment:</strong> keep a dated obligations register by jurisdiction, role and use case. Separate enacted duties, forthcoming application dates and consultations. This comparison explains the policy direction; it is not a complete compliance checklist.</p>
</section>
<section><h2 id="safety">07 / Safety: access and control need evidence</h2>
<p>Anthropic's separation of Fable and Mythos makes safeguards and verification part of the release itself. It reports prerelease testing that includes sandbox escapes. These are important disclosures, but a developer's assurance should be read as its assessment under stated tests, rather than a guarantee about a buyer's environment. <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/claude-fable-and-mythos-5-2">Safety and alignment section</a>.</p>
<p>The UK resilience proposal brings attention to a different layer: whether an organisation can detect and contain an agent that has departed from its intended task. That operational question remains relevant even when a model passes a capability evaluation. <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cyber-security-and-resilience-network-and-information-systems-bill-factsheets/agentic-ai-resilience-measures">UK loss-of-control measures</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Assessment:</strong> trial agents with narrow permissions, explicit approval for consequential actions and logs that connect instructions to outcomes. Test whether access can be revoked and work recovered after failure. A safety case should explain the remaining failure modes and who accepts them. Refusal rates alone cannot answer those questions.</p>
</section>
<section><h2 id="adoption">08 / Adoption: wider use, uneven depth</h2>
<p>The UK survey published on 29 October draws on 3,503 business interviews conducted between January and April. It reports adoption of at least one AI technology at 32%, with higher uptake among larger firms, at 52%. It also describes many businesses as early or exploratory users. This is a newly published view of earlier activity, not a live November reading. <a href={data.charts[1].source}>Survey methodology and findings</a>.</p>
<ReportBars {...data.charts[1]} />
<p>The Bank of England's October Agents' summary reports contacts moving from experimentation towards deployment at scale, particularly in professional and business services. This is qualitative intelligence from business contacts, not a representative estimate of the share of all firms deploying agents. It complements the survey without replacing its denominator. <a href="https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/agents-summary/2026/october-2026">Bank of England business conditions</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Assessment:</strong> distinguish having an account, using an embedded feature, redesigning a workflow and obtaining a sustained improvement. For an organisation's own rollout, report active use alongside quality, staff time and outcomes. Treat reported benefits as reported benefits until a measurement design supports stronger conclusions.</p>
</section>
<section><h2 id="watch">09 / What to watch in 2027</h2>
<p>These are editorial watchpoints, not predictions or announced release dates. Each follows a question raised by the evidence above.</p>
<ol class="watch"><li><strong>Cost per accepted job.</strong> Do cheaper execution models reduce the full cost after retries and review? Track task-level bills alongside the <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/introducing-gemini-4-hydrogen/">new pricing offers</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Agents under real permissions.</strong> Can deployments stop, recover and explain actions when a tool fails? Watch the <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cyber-security-and-resilience-network-and-information-systems-bill-factsheets/agentic-ai-resilience-measures">UK consultation</a> and publish task-level incident evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Open deployment economics.</strong> Do downloadable checkpoints deliver the required quality on affordable serving hardware? Test the <a href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B">actual artefact</a>, including its licence and runtime.</li>
<li><strong>Infrastructure delivery and cash.</strong> Compare delivered capacity and reported cash flows with earlier commitments. The <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/series-h">funding announcement</a> establishes intentions and agreements, not future returns.</li>
<li><strong>Regulatory preparation.</strong> Watch standards, guidance and enforcement capacity ahead of the <a href="https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/faqs/navigating-ai-act">EU's December 2027 high-risk date</a>. A later application date still leaves preparation work.</li>
<li><strong>Depth of adoption.</strong> Look for repeatable improvements and changes in work, rather than account counts alone. Future <a href={data.charts[1].source}>business survey waves</a> should be read with their fieldwork dates and populations intact.</li></ol>
</section>
<footer><h2>Sources and method</h2><p>All linked sources were checked through web retrieval on 14 November 2026. Sources may have been published earlier. Provider claims are attributed; analysis and recommendations are labelled. The report is a selected briefing, not an exhaustive release census. It uses no Artificial Analysis or LM Arena/Chatbot Arena data, including data quoted through another publisher. No composite capability ranking is calculated.</p><p>Charts use transcribed reported values with source URLs, units, periods and caveats in <code>src/data/reports/november-2026-charts.json</code>. Bars start at zero; the values remain readable without graphics or JavaScript. Dollar figures are US dollars. No currency conversion, inflation adjustment or annualisation is applied. Financing, revenue, loss and commitments remain separate.</p><p>Prepared with AI assistance and an editorial humaniser pass for UK English, attribution, plain language and unsupported certainty. This is not a claim of independent human review. Source and build evidence is retained with the PR. Draft only; publication requires a separate decision.</p></footer>
